{"id":"cd2785ff-744c-4cc3-b13d-8610c24517ef","object":"chat.completion","created":1784999055,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import json\\nfrom fractions import Fraction\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    \\n    min_sum = sum(s['minimum'] for s in segments)\\n    max_sum = sum(s['capacity'] for s in segments)\\n    if budget < min_sum or budget > max_sum:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Budget {budget} out of range [{min_sum}, {max_sum}]\\\")\\n    \\n    allocations = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\\n    remaining = budget - min_sum\\n    \\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best = None\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if allocations[i] < segments[i]['capacity']:\\n                prio = Fraction(segments[i]['importance'], allocations[i] + 1)\\n                if best is None or prio > best[0] or (prio == best[0] and i < best[1]):\\n                    best = (prio, i)\\n        if best is None:\\n            break\\n        idx = best[1]\\n        allocations[idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    \\n    by_name = {segments[i]['name']: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    return {\\n        'allocations': allocations,\\n        'by_name': by_name,\\n        'total': total\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements the described algorithm. The case payload has segments (list of dicts with name, importance, minimum, capacity) and budget. We must allocate tokens to segments subject to min and max capacities, using importance/next_position heuristic. Return allocations (list of integers in same order as segments), by_name (dict mapping segment name to allocated tokens), total (sum of allocations). Must handle tie-breaking by original segment order (i.e., when importance/next_pos equal, choose earlier segment). Must validate: budget >= sum(minimum) and budget <= sum(capacity). If not, raise ValueError. Also ensure each segment's allocated tokens between min and capacity.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Extract segments list and budget.\n- Compute sum_min = sum(s['minimum'] for s in segments)\n- Compute sum_max = sum(s['capacity'] for s in segments)\n- If budget < sum_min or budget > sum_max: raise ValueError\n- Initialize allocations list with min values.\n- remaining = budget - sum_min\n- While remaining > 0:\n    - For each segment i, compute priority = importance / (next_position) where next_position = allocations[i] + 1 (since next token would be at position allocations[i]+1). However algorithm says \"importance divided by its next allocated position\". So if currently allocated k tokens, the next token will be at position k+1. So priority = importance / (k+1). But need to ensure we don't exceed capacity. Only consider segments where allocations[i] < capacity.\n    - Find max priority; if multiple, pick the one with smallest index (original order). Then increment that segment's allocation by 1, remaining decreases by 1.\n- After loop, compute by_name dict from segment names and allocations.\n- total = budget (or sum allocations, same).\n- Return {\"allocations\": allocations, \"by_name\": by_name, \"total\": total}\n\nEdge cases: budget equal to sum_min -> no loop. Budget equal to sum_max -> fill all to capacity.\n\nNote: The algorithm described matches \"proportional allocation based on importance/position\" which is similar to a greedy priority queue.\n\nAlso need to ensure no mutation of input, but we are not mutating.\n\nWrite solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNow produce JSON response with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nMake sure to escape quotes, newlines, backslashes properly.\n\nImplementation code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    segments = case['segments']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(segments)\n    # Validate\n    min_sum = sum(s['minimum'] for s in segments)\n    max_sum = sum(s['capacity'] for s in segments)\n    if budget < min_sum or budget > max_sum:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget out of range\")\n    # Initialize allocations\n    allocations = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\n    remaining = budget - min_sum\n    # Greedy allocation\n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_idx = None\n        best_priority = -1.0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if allocations[i] < segments[i]['capacity']:\n                # next position = current + 1 (since we are about to allocate)\n                # Actually priority = importance / (allocations[i] + 1)\n                priority = segments[i]['importance'] / (allocations[i] + 1)\n                if priority > best_priority or (priority == best_priority and best_idx is None):\n                    best_priority = priority\n                    best_idx = i\n                # Tie-breaking: if equal priority, keep earlier index (we check later)\n            # For tie-breaking, we need to handle equal priority correctly:\n            # The current approach only updates when strictly greater or when best_idx is None.\n            # For equal priority, we need to keep the earlier index, which is already the case because we iterate in order and only replace if strictly greater. However, if later index has same priority, it won't replace because not greater. That's fine: we keep earlier.\n        if best_idx is None:\n            # Should not happen if budget <= max_sum, but just in case\n            break\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    # Build by_name\n    by_name = {s['name']: allocations[i] for i, s in enumerate(segments)}\n    total = sum(allocations)\n    return {\n        'allocations': allocations,\n        'by_name': by_name,\n        'total': total\n    }\n```\n\nBut note the tie-breaking: if equal priority, choose earlier index. The current logic keeps earlier index because it only updates on >. However, we need to ensure that if two have equal priority, the first encountered remains. That's fine. But careful: we compare priorities as float; could have tiny floating-point errors causing near-equal to not be equal. To be safe, we could use a tuple (priority, -index) but since we want earlier index, we can compare by (priority, -index) and pick the one with larger priority then smaller index? Actually, we want max priority, and for tie, smaller index. So we can compute key = (-priority, index) and pick min. Or simpler: store best priority and best index; if priority > best_priority or (priority == best_priority and i < best_idx). But we need to compare numbers. Use Python's fractions? Not necessary. Use float and compare with tolerance? Since input importance and allocations are integers, priority is rational. Could use a Fraction to avoid floating errors. But that might be overkill. However, specification says \"importance divided by its next allocated position\" implying rational division. Using float might risk tie-breaking if positions cause equal rational values but floating rounding causes them to differ (e.g., 1/3 vs 2/6). But importance and positions are integers, so rational numbers can be exactly compared using cross-multiplication: compare a/b vs c/d by a*d vs c*b. That would be safer. Let's implement with fractions or cross-multiplication. Use Python's Fraction from fractions module? It's standard library. To avoid external dependencies? Fraction is part of Python standard library. So we can use it. But note: we need to import Fraction inside solve. That's allowed. Use from fractions import Fraction. Then compare Fraction(importance, position). That ensures exact comparison and correct tie-breaking. However, we need to ensure we only consider segments that can accept more tokens. In the loop, for each segment i with capacity > allocations[i], compute priority = Fraction(segments[i]['importance'], allocations[i] + 1). Then find the max priority (and break ties by index). We can keep best_fraction and best_idx. If fraction > best_fraction, update. If equal, keep earlier (since we iterate in order, not update on equal). But with Fraction, equality is exact. So we can do:\n\nif frac > best_frac or (frac == best_frac and i < best_idx): but best_idx might be None initially. So simpler: keep best_frac None, and compare if best_frac is None or frac > best_frac or (frac == best_frac and i < best_idx). That ensures tie-breaking works. However, we need to update best_idx when we set best_frac for first time. So code:\n\nbest_frac = None\nbest_idx = -1\nfor i, seg in enumerate(segments):\n    if allocations[i] < seg['capacity']:\n        frac = Fraction(seg['importance'], allocations[i] + 1)\n        if best_frac is None or frac > best_frac or (frac == best_frac and i < best_idx):\n            best_frac = frac\n            best_idx = i\n\nBut note: if best_frac is None, then condition true and we set best_idx properly. Then for later indices with same fraction but larger index, condition (frac == best_frac and i < best_idx) will be false because i > best_idx, so they won't replace. That's correct.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: use tuple (-frac, i) and pick min. But -frac is tricky with Fraction (negation works). We can do:\n\ncandidates = [(-Fraction(seg['importance'], allocations[i] + 1), i) for i, seg in enumerate(segments) if allocations[i] < seg['capacity']]\nif not candidates: break\n_, best_idx = min(candidates)\n\nBut building list each iteration is fine for small n. We'll use that for clarity.\n\nWe also need to handle case when no candidate but remaining>0 -> shouldn't happen if budget<=max_sum, but safe.\n\nUsing Fraction import: ensure to import inside function.\n\nNow final code.\n\nReturn as JSON string.\n\nAlso include appropriate error handling: raise ValueError for invalid budget.\n\nNow produce final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1496,"completi